{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 4, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli ruled the Principality of Tripoli from 1154 until his death in 1199. He was a significant figure in the Crusades a powerful and ambitious ruler known for his military prowess political maneuvering and extensive building projects. His reign marked a period of relative stability and prosperity for Tripoli amidst ongoing conflicts in the Levant.\n\n*Early Life and Accession*\n\nRaymond was born around 1120 the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli. He inherited the county upon his father's death in 1154. His early years were marked by the challenges of maintaining Tripoli's independence in the face of threats from the Seljuk Turks and other Muslim powers. He skillfully navigated these threats forging alliances with other Crusader states and employing a combination of diplomacy and military force. His initial focus was on consolidating his authority within Tripoli and strengthening its defenses. He faced internal opposition from rival factions within the count's court a common challenge for rulers in the medieval period. He quickly established himself as a capable leader demonstrating both military skill and political acumen.\n\nRaymond III significantly expanded Tripoli's territory through military campaigns and strategic alliances. He engaged in frequent conflicts with the Zengids a powerful Muslim dynasty that controlled much of Syria. He also maintained relationships with the Knights Templar and other Crusader orders securing their support and utilizing their resources. His diplomacy was often characterized by pragmatism and a willingness to adapt to changing circumstances. He understood the importance of maintaining a strong military to deter aggression but also recognized the value of peaceful relations when possible.\n\n*Military Campaigns and Diplomacy*\n\nRaymond III was a skilled military commander leading numerous campaigns against his enemies. He successfully defended Tripoli from several Zengid invasions and played a key role in the Crusader defense of the Levant. He participated in several major Crusades including the Third Crusade where he allied with King Richard the Lionheart. He demonstrated his military capabilities in battles against Saladin's forces. His diplomatic efforts were equally important. He negotiated treaties with Muslim rulers securing trade routes and maintaining relative peace. He used diplomacy to prevent larger conflicts and to protect Tripoli's interests.\n\n*Building Projects and Legacy*\n\nRaymond III was a prolific builder leaving behind a legacy of impressive architectural monuments. He commissioned the construction of numerous churches mosques and fortifications throughout Tripoli. The Great Mosque of Tripoli a significant example of Islamic architecture was expanded during his reign. He also oversaw the construction of new walls and towers strengthening Tripoli's defenses. His building projects not only served practical purposes but also reflected his ambition and his desire to enhance the prestige of his county. He fostered a vibrant cultural and economic environment contributing to Tripoli's prosperity. Raymond III died in 1199 leaving behind a stable and prosperous county. He is remembered as one of the most successful and influential rulers of Tripoli a capable military leader and a shrewd diplomat. His reign represents a high point in the history of the county.\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.0021344278939068317}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -3.099436753473128e-06}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁ruled", "logprob": -0.29277363419532776}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.39782649278640747}, {"token": "▁Princip", "logprob": -1.946076512336731}, {"token": "ality", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -1.9073468138230965e-06}, {"token"